bench:qa:96.answer_text:
Providers of high-risk AI systems must establish, implement, document and maintain a quality‑management system (QMS) to ensure ongoing compliance with the AI Act. The QMS must be proportionate to the nature of the provider and the type of high‑risk system, and it must cover the provider’s organisational structure, responsibilities and procedures across the system’s lifecycle.

In particular, the QMS must include documented policies and procedures for (at least):
- compliance strategy and regulatory compliance processes;
- design control and development procedures (including requirements management);
- data management (including data governance measures relevant to training/validation/testing where applicable);
- risk management and post‑market monitoring arrangements;
- technical documentation and record‑keeping;
- testing, verification and validation procedures;
- change management and configuration control;
- incident handling and corrective/preventive actions;
- control of outsourced processes and supplier/third‑party management;
- internal audits and management review, including continuous improvement.

Providers must keep the QMS up to date, ensure it is effectively implemented, and be able to demonstrate it to competent authorities/notified bodies as part of conformity assessment and market surveillance activities.

bench:qa:14.answer_text:
Under Article 10, high‑risk AI systems that use training techniques must be developed using training, validation and testing data sets that meet the Article 10(2)–(5) quality criteria. In particular, Article 10(3) requires those data sets to be:
- relevant;
- sufficiently representative;
- to the best extent possible, free of errors and complete for the system’s intended purpose; and
- to have appropriate statistical properties, including (where applicable) regarding the persons or groups of persons the system is intended to be used on.
These characteristics can be satisfied by individual data sets or by a combination of data sets.

bench:qa:17.answer_text:
Under Article 12(2), high‑risk AI systems must have logging capabilities that enable recording events relevant to ensuring appropriate traceability of the system’s functioning for its intended purpose. In particular, the logs must support recording events relevant for:
(a) identifying situations that may result in the system presenting a risk (within the meaning of Article 79(1)) or that may amount to a substantial modification;
(b) facilitating post‑market monitoring (Article 72);
(c) monitoring the operation of certain high‑risk AI systems (those referred to in Article 26(5)).

bench:qa:77.answer_text:
The purpose of the fundamental-rights impact assessment (FRIA) for certain high-risk AI systems is to ensure fundamental rights are effectively protected by requiring the deployer, before putting the system into use, to:

- identify the specific risks to the fundamental rights of individuals or groups likely to be affected in the конкрет context of use;
- identify and plan measures to be taken if those risks materialise (e.g., governance arrangements, human oversight, complaint handling and redress);
- document key aspects of the intended deployment context (processes where the system will be used, duration/frequency of use, and categories of persons/groups likely to be affected), and update the assessment when relevant factors change.

Where relevant, the FRIA is meant to complement an existing data protection impact assessment (DPIA) rather than duplicate it.

bench:qa:25.answer_text:
Before making a high-risk AI system available on the market, distributors must verify that:
- it bears the required CE marking;
- it is accompanied by a copy of the EU declaration of conformity (Article 47);
- it is accompanied by instructions for use; and
- the provider and (as applicable) the importer have complied with their respective obligations under Article 16(b) and (c) and Article 23(3).

bench:qa:30.answer_text:
When applying for notification, a conformity‑assessment body’s application must be accompanied by:
- a description of the conformity‑assessment activities;
- the conformity‑assessment module or modules;
- the types of AI systems for which it claims to be competent;
- an accreditation certificate (where one exists) issued by a national accreditation body attesting it fulfils the requirements in Article 31; and
- any valid document related to existing designations of the applicant notified body under other Union harmonisation legislation (to be added).

bench:qa:74.answer_text:
Under Article 13(3), the instructions for use of a high‑risk AI system must contain at least:

- The identity and contact details of the provider and, where applicable, its authorised representative.
- The characteristics, capabilities and limitations of performance of the high‑risk AI system, including:
  - its intended purpose;
  - the level of accuracy (including metrics), robustness and cybersecurity against which it has been tested/validated and which can be expected, and known/foreseeable circumstances that may affect those levels;
  - any known or foreseeable circumstances (including reasonably foreseeable misuse) that may lead to risks to health and safety or fundamental rights;
  - where applicable, the system’s technical capabilities/characteristics to provide information relevant to explain its output;
  - when appropriate, its performance regarding specific persons or groups of persons on which it is intended to be used;
  - when appropriate, specifications for input data, or other relevant information about the training/validation/testing datasets used, taking into account the intended purpose;
  - where applicable, information enabling deployers to interpret the output and use it appropriately.
- Any pre-determined changes to the high‑risk AI system and its performance (set by the provider at the moment of the initial conformity assessment), if any.
- The human oversight measures (including technical measures facilitating deployers’ interpretation of outputs).
- The computational and hardware resources needed, the expected lifetime, and necessary maintenance/care measures (including frequency) to ensure proper functioning, including software updates.
- Where relevant, a description of the logging mechanisms that allow deployers to properly collect, store and interpret logs in accordance with Article 12.

bench:qa:20.answer_text:
Under the EU AI Act, the purpose of human oversight for high‑risk AI systems is to ensure that the system is provided and used in a way that enables the natural persons assigned oversight to:
- understand the system’s relevant capacities and limitations and monitor its operation, including to detect and address anomalies, dysfunctions and unexpected performance;
- remain aware of and mitigate automation bias (the tendency to automatically rely or over‑rely on the system’s output);
- correctly interpret the system’s output;
- decide, in a particular situation, not to use the system or to disregard, override or reverse its output; and
- intervene in the system’s operation or interrupt it (e.g., via a stop button) so it can come to a halt in a safe state.

The Act also requires that instructions for use include the human oversight measures (including technical measures facilitating interpretation of outputs), supporting these oversight purposes in practice.

bench:qa:25.answer_text:
