bench:qa:56.answer_text:
Testing high‑risk AI systems in real‑world conditions outside AI regulatory sandboxes may be conducted by providers or prospective providers of Annex III high‑risk AI systems, but only in accordance with Article 60 and a “real‑world testing plan”, and without prejudice to the Article 5 prohibitions. It is also without prejudice to any Union/national rules on real‑world testing for product‑related high‑risk AI systems covered by Annex I harmonisation legislation, and to any ethical review required by Union or national law.

Providers/prospective providers may conduct such testing only if all Article 60(4) conditions are met, including in particular:
- draw up a real‑world testing plan and submit it to the market surveillance authority (MSA) in the Member State where testing will occur;
- obtain MSA approval of the testing and the plan (tacit approval after 30 days unless national law excludes tacit approval, in which case an authorisation is required);
- register the real‑world testing in the relevant EU database section (public or secure/non‑public, depending on the Annex III category/area), with a Union‑wide unique single identification number and required information;
- be established in the Union or appoint an EU‑established legal representative;
- transfer testing data to third countries only with appropriate/applicable Union‑law safeguards;
- limit duration to what is necessary and in any case max 6 months (extendable once by 6 months with prior notification and justification);
- appropriately protect subjects from vulnerable groups (age/disability);
- if cooperating with deployers/prospective deployers: inform them of relevant aspects, provide relevant instructions for use, and conclude an agreement allocating roles/responsibilities to ensure compliance;
- obtain subjects’ informed consent (or, for law enforcement where consent would prevent testing: ensure no negative effect on subjects and delete their personal data after the test);
- ensure effective oversight by suitably qualified persons with capacity/training/authority;
- ensure AI outputs can be effectively reversed and disregarded.

bench:qa:74.answer_text:
For a high‑risk AI system, the **instructions for use** must contain **at least** the following information:

- **Provider identification**: the identity and contact details of the provider and, where applicable, its authorised representative.
- **Characteristics, capabilities and performance limitations**, including:
  - the system’s **intended purpose**;
  - the **level of accuracy** (including metrics), **robustness** and **cybersecurity** against which it has been tested/validated, the expected levels, and known/foreseeable circumstances that may affect those levels;
  - any known/foreseeable circumstances (including reasonably foreseeable misuse) that may lead to **risks to health and safety or fundamental rights**;
  - where applicable, the system’s technical capabilities/characteristics to provide information relevant to **explain its output**;
  - when appropriate, performance regarding **specific persons or groups** on which it is intended to be used;
  - when appropriate, **specifications for input data** and/or other relevant information about the **training, validation and testing datasets** used (taking into account intended purpose);
  - where applicable, information enabling deployers to **interpret the output and use it appropriately**.
- **Pre-determined changes**: any changes to the system and its performance that were pre-determined by the provider at the time of the initial conformity assessment (if any).
- **Human oversight measures** (Article 14): including technical measures to facilitate deployers’ interpretation of outputs.
- **Resources, lifetime, maintenance**: computational and hardware resources needed, expected lifetime, and necessary maintenance/care measures (including frequency) to ensure proper functioning, including software updates.
- **Logging mechanisms (where relevant)**: a description of mechanisms that allow deployers to properly collect, store and interpret logs in accordance with Article 12.

bench:qa:23.answer_text:
A provider must appoint an authorised representative (established in the Union, by written mandate) when the provider is established in a third country:
- for high-risk AI systems: prior to making the high-risk AI system available on the Union market;
- for general-purpose AI models: prior to placing the general-purpose AI model on the Union market.

bench:qa:24.answer_text:
Before placing a high‑risk AI system on the market, importers shall ensure the system is in conformity with the Regulation by verifying that:
- the relevant conformity assessment procedure (Article 43) has been carried out by the provider;
- the provider has drawn up the technical documentation in accordance with Article 11 and Annex IV;
- the system bears the required CE marking and is accompanied by the EU declaration of conformity (Article 47) and instructions for use;
- the provider has appointed an authorised representative in accordance with Article 22(1).

