bench:qa:56.answer_text:
Testing high‑risk AI systems in real‑world conditions outside AI regulatory sandboxes may be conducted by providers or prospective providers of Annex III high‑risk AI systems, in accordance with Article 60 and a “real‑world testing plan”, and without prejudice to Article 5 prohibitions and any applicable Union/national product‑testing law (e.g., for Annex I harmonisation‑legislation products). It is also without prejudice to any ethical review required by Union or national law.

Providers/prospective providers may conduct such testing only if all Article 60(4) conditions are met, including in particular:
- draw up a real‑world testing plan and submit it to the market surveillance authority (MSA) of the Member State where testing will occur;
- obtain MSA approval of the testing and plan (tacit approval after 30 days unless national law excludes tacit approval, in which case an authorisation is required);
- register the testing in the EU database with a Union‑wide unique single identification number and required information (with specific registration routes/exceptions for certain Annex III systems, including law enforcement/migration/border management and Annex III(2));
- be established in the Union or appoint an EU‑established legal representative;
- transfer testing data to third countries only with appropriate/applicable Union‑law safeguards;
- limit duration to what is necessary and in any case max 6 months (extendable once by 6 months with prior notification and justification to the MSA);
- appropriately protect subjects who are vulnerable due to age or disability;
- if testing is done with deployers/prospective deployers: inform them of relevant aspects, provide relevant instructions for use, and conclude an agreement allocating roles/responsibilities to ensure compliance;
- obtain subjects’ informed consent in accordance with Article 61, or (for law enforcement where consent would prevent testing) ensure no negative effect on subjects and delete their personal data after the test;
- ensure effective oversight by suitably qualified persons with necessary capacity/training/authority (including by deployers/prospective deployers where applicable);
- ensure AI outputs can be effectively reversed and disregarded.

